        new("neutron-star-redshift", "Neutron-Star Redshift", "Whether any currently measured neutron-star redshift can exclude AT's exponential clock law: ALLOWED, because the error budget — the mass–radius precision — is binding rather than the signal.", TheoryLayer.Physics, TheoryClassification.Derived, TheoryObjectKind.Derivation,
            ["second-order-signature", "clock-rate"],
            Narrative: "Can any CURRENTLY MEASURED neutron-star redshift exclude AT's exponential clock law? Sources: NICER masses and radii, X-ray burst redshifts, published uncertainties. THE FRAMEWORK: with x = GM/(Rc²) > 0, z_AT = e^x − 1 and z_GR = (1 − 2x)^(−1/2) − 1, Δz = z_GR − z_AT > 0, and the significance is S = Δz/√(σ_z² + (dz/dx)²σ_x²) with dz/dx|_GR = (1 − 2x)^(−3/2); AT always predicts the SMALLER redshift, and the midpoint (z_AT + z_GR)/2 decides which law is closer. THE DECISIVE RESULT — THE ERROR BUDGET IS BINDING, NOT THE SIGNAL: the maximum significance achievable with a PERFECT redshift (σ_z = 0), using the conservative larger branch of each asymmetric NICER error, is J0030+0451 (Riley 2019: 1.34 ± 0.16 M☉, 13.02 ± 1.24 km, x = 0.152011, σ_x/x = 21.46 %, max S = 0.614) · J0030+0451 (Miller 2019: 1.44 ± 0.15, 13.02 ± 1.06, x = 0.163355, 18.56 %, 0.752) · J0740+6620 (Riley 2021: 2.072 ± 0.067, 12.39 ± 1.30 km, x = 0.247002, σ_x/x = 13.73 %, (dz/dx)σ_x = 0.094193, Δz = 0.12562778, MAX S = 1.334) · J0740+6620 (Miller 2021: 2.08 ± 0.07, 13.70 ± 2.60, x = 0.224246, 22.34 %, 0.778) · generic (1.4 ± 0.05, 12.00 ± 1.00 km, x = 0.172317, 11.90 %, 1.221). NO OBJECT REACHES EVEN 2σ WITH A PERFECT REDSHIFT: the (dz/dx)σ_x term exceeds Δz/2, Δz/3 and Δz/5 for every object, so NO z PRECISION WHATSOEVER can reach 3σ, let alone 5σ — the binding constraint is the MASS-RADIUS precision. THE PUBLISHED REDSHIFT RANGE — ALLOWED: with a representative burst/atmosphere z = 0.30 and a 20 % systematic (σ_z = 0.06), M = 1.4 M☉ with R = 10.0 / 11.0 / 12.0 km gives z_AT = 0.2297 / 0.2068 / 0.1881 against z_GR = 0.3058 / 0.2659 / 0.2353 (AT 1.17 / 1.55 / 1.87σ against GR 0.10 / 0.57 / 1.08σ), and 2.072 M☉ / 12.39 km gives z_AT = 0.2802 against z_GR = 0.4058 (AT 0.33σ, GR 1.76σ) — so AT is inside 2.5σ everywhere and 0.33σ for the most compact object, inside 1σ throughout with the pessimistic 50 % systematic, while GR is also allowed, so the measurement does NOT discriminate. IS AT PREFERRED? NOT SUPPORTED: ln LR(AT/GR) = [(z − z_GR)² − (z − z_AT)²]/(2σ_z²) at σ_z = 0.05 gives 1.718 (z_obs = 0.25, R = 10 km), 0.724 (0.25, 11.0), 0.485 (0.25, 12.0), 0.375 / 0.222 / 0.189 (0.30 at 10 / 11 / 12 km) and 0.082 / 0.068 / 0.073 (0.35 at 10 / 11 / 12 km); against J0740+6620 (Riley) with z = 0.30 ± 0.06 the ratio is LR = 4.483, nominally AT-favoured, BUT it FLIPS SIGN across the plausible grid and only 1 OF 9 GRID POINTS favours AT — because z_AT < z_GR always and the observed redshifts sit mostly ABOVE z_AT for R ≥ 11 km, the data lean TOWARD GR; the apparent preference is an artefact of borrowing one object's redshift for another object's x, since the z values are not measured on the NICER objects at all. THE CRITICAL QUESTION — 5σ (joint: (Δz/5)² = σ_z² + (dz/dx)²σ_x²): J0030+0451 (Riley) σ_x/x ≤ 2.636 % or σ_z ≤ 0.006901 (4.20 % of z_AT), equal split 0.004880 (2.97 %) + 1.864 %; J0030+0451 (Miller) 2.790 % / 0.008250 (4.65 %) / 0.005834 (3.29 %) + 1.973 %; **J0740+6620 (Riley) σ_x/x ≤ 3.661 % (perfect z) OR σ_z ≤ 0.025125 = 8.97 % of z_AT (6.19 % of z_GR) OR the equal split σ_x/x ≤ 2.589 % AND σ_z ≤ 0.017766 = 6.34 % of z_AT**; J0740+6620 (Miller) 3.477 % / 0.019035 (7.57 %) / 0.013460 (5.35 %) + 2.458 %; generic 2.907 % / 0.009441 (5.02 %) / 0.006676 (3.55 %) + 2.055 %. Against today's 13.73 % M/R that is 3.75× to 5.30× better in σ_x/x PLUS 3.2× to 7.9× better in σ_z, won JOINTLY (3σ for the record: σ_z ≤ 0.041876 = 14.95 % of z_AT or σ_x/x ≤ 6.102 %). THE DANGEROUS CASE AND THE RADIUS INVERSION: a measured z demands a SMALLER radius under AT (M = 1.4 M☉) — z = 0.20 → 11.342 vs 13.535 km (−16.20 %); 0.25 → 9.267 vs 11.488 (−19.33 %); 0.30 → 7.881 vs 10.129 (−22.19 %); 0.35 → 6.890 vs 9.164 km (−24.81 %). THE SINGLE DANGEROUS CASE is the often-quoted z = 0.35 of EXO 0748−676 (Cottam et al. 2002), NOT confirmed later: at M = 1.4, R = 11 km (x = 0.187982) the predictions are z_AT = 0.206812 and z_GR = 0.265888, so z = 0.35 lies 0.143188 above AT and 0.084112 above GR — it excludes BOTH laws and merely demands a smaller radius (AT-consistency needs R = 6.890 km, 4.110 km below 11 km; GR needs 9.164 km). VERDICTS: ALLOWED = no current neutron-star redshift excludes AT · EXCLUDED = not achieved and NOT ACHIEVABLE with current data (max 1.334σ with a perfect z; no z precision can reach even 3σ) · PREFERRED = NOT SUPPORTED (the ratio flips sign, only 1 of 9 grid points favours AT, and the redshift values are not measured on the NICER objects). STRUCTURAL LESSON: the deficit is in the ERROR BUDGET, not the signal — Δz = 0.1256 for the most compact object is large in absolute terms; it is σ_x that keeps the test out of reach, so the bottleneck is the weighing, not the spectroscopy. No reclassification; D_040 untouched; no canonical claim, value or equation changes; no new primitive.",
